> How does this work? You kick the sink repeatedly to get puddings, kill
> them and sac them? Where's the benefit of this, do they drop good items?

You only get one pudding per sink.  But if you hit (but don't kill) a
pudding, sometimes it will divide into two.  So if you attack puddings with
a weak weapon (a knife that you've dulled by engraving works well), you can
create an unlimited supply of puddings.  This will give you some items; but
best of all is to do this on a level with an altar, and gain an unlimited
supply of sacrifice fodder.

> >  Liberal usage
> > of the Drain Life spell got my equipment all to +5/+7 (and
> > disenchanters were genocided in order to keep it this way).
>
> How does drain life enhance equipment?

It doesn't.  If you zap yourself with drain life, it will drain plusses
from your equipment.  This is useful because of the maximum enchantments on
equipment.  For example, it's not safe to enchant most armor when it's
enchanted to higher than +3.  So if you have a helmet that is at +4, you
drain it to +3, then read a blessed scroll of enchant armor to (hopefully)
bring it up to +5.

